    id: "04d3b219a1d2ffdb93cf",
    cat: "geopolitica",
    title: "Alleged rebel leader killed in Colombia as president targets armed groups",
    summary: "The military operation against Hernando Forero Mosquera was carried out just days after Colombia's new president was sworn in.",
    preview: "Alleged rebel leader killed in Colombia as president targets armed groups - Published A man suspected of leading a dissident faction of a left-wing rebel group has been killed in a military operation in central Colombia, officials say. The operation forms part of a crackdown on illegal armed groups ordered by Colombia's new president, Abelardo de la Espriella, who was sworn in on Friday.",
    body: "Alleged rebel leader killed in Colombia as president targets armed groups\n\n- Published\n\nA man suspected of leading a dissident faction of a left-wing rebel group has been killed in a military operation in central Colombia, officials say.\n\nThe operation forms part of a crackdown on illegal armed groups ordered by Colombia's new president, Abelardo de la Espriella, who was sworn in on Friday.\n\nHe said the killing by the security forces of Hernando Forero Mosquera and five other alleged members of armed groups on the weekend sent \"a clear and powerful message to all bandits that there is nowhere for them to hide\".\n\nDuring his campaign for the presidency, de la Espriella said he would target \"narco-terrorism\" relentlessly, including by launching air strikes.\n\nMilitary intelligence officers said that Forero led a group known as Frente 28, which is one of the many dissident factions to have sprung forth from the Revolutionary Armed Forces of Colombia (Farc).\n\nThe Farc, Colombia's largest left-wing rebel group, signed a peace deal with the government in 2016 but some of its members did not agree to the terms of the deal and refused to lay down their arms.\n\nThese dissidents groups, which are now mainly involved in cocaine trafficking and illegal mining, are among the main driving forces behind the resurgence in violence in the country.\n\nDe la Espriella said he would take a radically different approach to defeating criminal groups in the country than his predecessor in office, Gustavo Petro.\n\nPetro, the first left-wing politician to be elected president of Colombia, advocated what he called a \"total peace\" strategy, which prioritised negotiation over confrontation.\n\nHis attempts to negotiate with a number of armed groups, however, failed as members of the illegal organisations repeatedly broke ceasefires and walked away from the talks.\n\nDe la Espriella's combative approach has been met with defiance by dissident rebel groups.\n\nOn Saturday, just a day after the was sworn into office, two explosive devices went off.\n\nIn Cauca province, a car bomb exploded at a tollbooth, injuring two security guards, and in Cesar province, a police officer was killed by explosives dropped from a drone.\n\nIn a video uploaded to social media, de la Espriella praised the military for killing Forero, who was also known as \"El ruso\" (The Russian), and whom he described as an explosives expert.\n\nHe added that three more alleged members of the dissident group had been killed in the military operation in a rural area in Meta province.\n\nThe president also said that soldiers had killed two alleged members of another criminal group, the Gulf Clan, in a separate offensive in Antioquia province.\n\nDe la Espriella has the backing of US President Donald Trump, who endorsed his candidacy.\n\nOn Friday, the US state department said it planned to provide $1bn (£740m) in security assistance to de la Espriella's government.\n\nIt also welcomed Colombia into the US-led group dubbed \"Shield of the Americas\" - an alliance of Western Hemisphere countries which have pledged to co-operate in their fight against drug trafficking gangs and transnational crime.",
